INSERT INTO main_app_briefing VALUES(34,'stocks','2026-09-13 08:17:49.084668','```json',replace('```json\n{\n  "headline": "US stocks decline as Treasury yields hit new highs amid inflation concerns",\n  "quiet": false,\n  "items": [\n    {\n      "text": "On Thursday, U.S. stocks declined as Treasury yields soared due to rising inflationary pressures and surging oil prices. The 2-year and 10-year Treasury yields reached their highest levels since mid-2024 and 2023, respectively, in response to an increase in the Producer Price Index (PPI), which rose 0.4% month-over-month and 5.4% year-over-year, largely driven by energy price spikes tied to the Iran war. Core PPI also increased, highlighting persistent inflation. These developments are fueling expectations that the Federal Reserve may raise interest rates at next week’s meeting, with futures markets pricing in a 71% chance of a quarter-point hike. Major indexes, including the Dow Jones, S&P 500, and Nasdaq, fell for the fourth consecutive day, though gains in mega-cap stocks such as Apple, Alphabet, and SpaceX mitigated the losses. In corporate news, AeroVironment surged 4.5% following strong earnings, while Oracle dipped ahead of its earnings report despite anticipated growth. Meanwhile, Argan fell 1.4% despite announcing a 40% dividend increase, though the stock remains up 29% year-to-date. Investors await the August Consumer Price Index report, which could influence the Fed''s upcoming rate decision.",\n      "url": "https://www.kiplinger.com/investing/stocks/stocks-drop-as-treasury-yields-hit-new-highs-stock-market-today"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent''s recent attempt to stabilize the bond market by significantly increasing government bond buybacks fell short, as interest rates continued to rise. The Treasury announced plans to triple its buyback operations to $6 billion per round this quarter, aiming to ease bond yields. Despite this move, the yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note spiked to 4.85%, the highest level since October 2023, before slightly retreating to 4.84%. This reaction implies skepticism from investors and suggests that Bessent''s aggressive strategy may be counterproductive, potentially driving rates higher. Analysts, including Guneet Dhingra of BNP Paribas, criticized the measure as a temporary fix that does not address deeper fiscal challenges like the U.S. structural deficit, contrasting Bessent’s limited tools with the broader authority used by former Treasury Secretary Hank Paulson during the 2008 financial crisis.",\n      "url": "https://www.axios.com/2026/09/10/bessent-bonds-buyback-treasury"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "In August 2026, U.S. home sales declined to their slowest annual pace in over a year, with existing home sales falling 2% from July to an annual rate of 3.98 million units, according to the National Association of Realtors. This marks the third consecutive monthly decrease and a 1.2% drop compared to August 2025. Rising mortgage rates—now averaging 6.76%, the highest in over 14 months—have made home buying less affordable, contributing to the market slowdown. Home prices, however, continue to rise, with the national median sales price reaching $429,100, an all-time high for August. The housing market has remained sluggish since 2022 due to increasing borrowing costs and a persistent shortage of homes for sale, despite a slight increase in inventory to 1.62 million units. This has led to properties staying on the market longer, with the current supply level at 4.9 months, the highest in over a decade. Regional sales were down in the Northeast, Midwest, and South, while the West remained flat. Affordability challenges persist, with first-time buyers making up only 30% of sales, well below the historical average.",\n      "url": "https://apnews.com/article/f6805f57c7e0f147592357f8e378030d"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "In August 2026, retail investors showed diminished bullishness towards the stock market, reflected in a 3.9% decline in Charles Schwab’s Trading Activity Index (STAX), which dipped from a four-year high of 59.80 in July to 57.50. This reduced enthusiasm occurred despite early-month market gains and amid rising Treasury yields and surging energy prices, potentially signaling growing investor caution in a prolonged bull market. Joe Mazzola, Schwab’s head trading and derivatives strategist, noted a shift in retail behavior toward exchange-traded funds (ETFs) over individual stocks. Nevertheless, Elon Musk''s SpaceX emerged as the top single-stock favorite among retail investors. Looking ahead, a separate survey indicates that Schwab clients regained some bullish sentiment approaching September.",\n      "url": "https://www.axios.com/2026/09/08/schwab-retail-investors-august"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "On Friday, September 11, 2026, U.S. stock markets rebounded, recovering much of their weekly losses as oil prices declined and inflation data met expectations, easing investor concerns. The S&P 500 climbed 0.9% to 7,656.98, the Dow Jones Industrial Average increased 1% to 52,573.29, and the Nasdaq rose 1% to 26,333.04. The Russell 2000, which tracks smaller companies, rose 0.4% to 2,903.94. The drop in Brent oil prices by nearly 3% alleviated inflation worries and contributed to market optimism. Despite Friday''s gains, all major indexes were down ','\n',char(10)),'[]',1,'gpt-4o-mini',0.011959999999999999431,'');
