INSERT INTO main_app_briefing VALUES(38,'stocks','2026-09-13 12:18:14.298439','```json',replace('```json\n{\n  "headline": "US stock market rebounds as oil prices retreat and inflation data cools",\n  "quiet": false,\n  "items": [\n    {\n      "text": "US stock markets rebounded strongly on Friday, September 11, 2026, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average surging 509 points (1%) to 52,573, breaking a four-day losing streak. The S&P 500 and Nasdaq also gained 0.9% and 1.0%, respectively. The rally was fueled by declining oil prices and investor bargain hunting, despite inflation data indicating rising consumer prices. The August Consumer Price Index (CPI) rose 0.4% month-over-month and 3.4% year-over-year, driven largely by a 3.9% monthly increase in gasoline prices. Core inflation, excluding food and energy, rose 0.3% monthly and 2.4% annually, aligning with economists’ expectations. Expectations for an interest rate hike increased, with CME Group data showing an 86% probability of a 25 basis-point hike by the Federal Reserve next week. Despite oil futures falling 2.7% to $99.68 per barrel, diesel prices hit $6 per gallon. Notable market movers included Dell Technologies, which soared 12% following a bullish RBC analyst report, and Cisco Systems, up 4.4%, making it the top Dow performer. Oracle shares dipped 1.7% despite strong earnings, possibly due to profit-taking after recent gains. Overall, market sentiment improved amid mixed macroeconomic signals and tech sector developments.",\n      "url": "https://www.kiplinger.com/investing/stocks/dow-soars-509-points-as-oil-prices-retreat-stock-market-today"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "Wholesale inflation in the U.S. rose to 5.4% in August 2026, up from 4.8% in July, primarily driven by surging energy prices linked to the ongoing Iran conflict. Monthly prices increased by 0.4%, up from 0.1% in the prior month. Diesel prices soared 24.1% in one month and 78% year-over-year, influencing broader cost increases in manufacturing and shipping. Core inflation, excluding volatile food and energy costs, rose 0.2% month-over-month and 4.6% annually. Airfares, hospital care, and electronic component prices also jumped due to increased demand, particularly from AI investments. Meanwhile, food prices remained mostly flat, and electric utilities saw declining prices. With U.S. oil prices surpassing $100 per barrel and gasoline up 44% since February, inflation persists. President Trump acknowledged that oil prices may not ease until after the November midterm elections. The Federal Reserve is closely monitoring inflation, with potential interest rate hikes under consideration depending on forthcoming consumer price index data. Fed Chair Kevin Warsh emphasized the need for inflation to return to 2%, while some officials, including Governor Christopher Waller, indicated a willingness to hold rates steady if inflation continues to ease. The next CPI report is expected to heavily influence the Fed’s rate decision.",\n      "url": "https://apnews.com/article/66d1591b979271ee7b79b113d715f6ef"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "On Thursday, U.S. stocks declined as Treasury yields soared due to rising inflationary pressures and surging oil prices. The 2-year and 10-year Treasury yields reached their highest levels since mid-2024 and 2023, respectively, in response to an increase in the Producer Price Index (PPI), which rose 0.4% month-over-month and 5.4% year-over-year, largely driven by energy price spikes tied to the Iran war. Core PPI also increased, highlighting persistent inflation. These developments are fueling expectations that the Federal Reserve may raise interest rates at next week’s meeting, with futures markets pricing in a 71% chance of a quarter-point hike. Major indexes, including the Dow Jones, S&P 500, and Nasdaq, fell for the fourth consecutive day, though gains in mega-cap stocks such as Apple, Alphabet, and SpaceX mitigated the losses. In corporate news, AeroVironment surged 4.5% following strong earnings, while Oracle dipped ahead of its earnings report despite anticipated growth. Meanwhile, Argan fell 1.4% despite announcing a 40% dividend increase, though the stock remains up 29% year-to-date. Investors await the August Consumer Price Index report, which could influence the Fed''s upcoming rate decision.",\n      "url": "https://www.kiplinger.com/investing/stocks/stocks-drop-as-treasury-yields-hit-new-highs-stock-market-today"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "Since April 2025, small-cap stocks, as represented by the Russell 2000 Index, have experienced a significant rally with a 48.1% return, outperforming the S&P 500’s 35.6%. The iShares Core S&P Small-Cap ETF (IJR), which emphasizes profitability, also posted a notable 41.4% gain, although much of the early rally benefited unprofitable firms. The surge is partially attributed to enthusiasm around artificial intelligence (AI), which is creating opportunities for small firms that supply essential components and services, including semiconductor makers, energy providers, and construction companies. The Core S&P Small-Cap ETF has seen strong contributions from technology, healthcare, and industrial sectors, including companies like Viasat and Protagonist Therapeutics. While small-cap rallies have historically been short-lived, recent trends indicate improved profitability and strong earnings growth potential. Analysts forecast 54% earnings growth for Russell 2000 companies in 2026, significantly outp ','\n',char(10)),'[]',1,'gpt-4o-mini',0.011959999999999999431,'');
