INSERT INTO main_app_briefing VALUES(90,'stocks','2026-09-15 16:23:24.530104','```json',replace('```json\n{\n  "headline": "US stocks decline amid rising oil prices and bond yields",\n  "quiet": false,\n  "items": [\n    {\n      "text": "US stock markets declined under pressure from surging oil prices and rising bond yields. The S&P 500 slipped 0.4%, the Dow Jones dropped 440 points (0.8%), and the Nasdaq composite fell 0.7%. The 10-year Treasury yield reached 4.99%, briefly topping 5.04%, its highest level since 2023, raising borrowing costs and reducing investor appetite for stocks in favor of safer bonds. Market volatility intensified following higher oil prices triggered by the ongoing war with Iran, pushing Brent crude to $108.12 per barrel. Concerns about prolonged inflation and the U.S. government''s high debt levels compounded economic anxiety. The Federal Reserve is expected to raise interest rates this week for the first time in three years. Consumer-focused stocks such as Chipotle (-5.5%), United Airlines (-3.5%), and Dollar Tree (-4.2%) were notably impacted, while Dave & Buster''s plunged 17.2% after weak earnings. Meanwhile, artificial intelligence stocks like Nvidia (+0.6%) and AMD (+2%) partially rebounded following a previous day slump due to safety concerns in AI development. International markets also experienced declines, though less severe, with South Korea''s Kospi index falling 0.9%.",\n      "url": "https://apnews.com/article/123ddc2d8ba06c324cbbd899f7eeebd2"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "Concerns over the potential dangers of advanced AI technologies are leading to a surge in cybersecurity stock values, as investors anticipate heightened demand for security solutions. High-profile figures like Anthropic’s Dario Amodei, OpenAI’s Sam Altman, and SpaceX’s Elon Musk recently advocated for a slowdown in developing cutting-edge AI models to address safety risks, a call that has intensified fears among investors. As a result, stocks in AI-related industries, such as semiconductor companies, declined, while cybersecurity companies like CrowdStrike and Palo Alto Networks saw their shares climb by approximately 14% and 13%, respectively. This shift reflects growing sentiment that businesses and governments must significantly ramp up cybersecurity spending to manage AI-driven threats. Gartner projects global spending in AI cybersecurity to reach $51.3 billion in 2026 and $86 billion by 2027, although this remains a small portion of the larger $2.5 trillion in overall AI investments. Industry leaders note that public fears are increasing awareness, which serves as a catalyst for cybersecurity growth. However, experts caution that while fears of AI-driven catastrophe persist, the primary concern for investors is the potential for security breaches and operational failures with tangible financial consequences.",\n      "url": "https://www.axios.com/2026/09/15/ai-cybersecurity-stocks"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "Despite mounting concerns from AI industry leaders about the rapid advancement of artificial intelligence and the threat posed by uncontrolled superintelligence, Wall Street remains largely unfazed. On Monday, equity investors showed little reaction to warnings of an AI-induced existential crisis. While chipmaker stocks declined, the Nasdaq, heavily weighted with AI companies, only dipped by 0.6%. Experts suggest that President Trump''s dismissal of regulatory slowdowns assured investors, who seem more influenced by the optimistic outlook of executives like Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang than by cautionary voices like Sam Altman of OpenAI and Dario Amodei of Anthropic. Gene Munster of Deepwater Asset Management noted that competitive pressures, especially between the U.S. and China, make it unlikely that major AI firms will slow development. This response reflects a longer pattern of Wall Street prioritizing market dynamics over speculative futuristic threats, leaving open the question of whether this is a miscalculation or a rational response.",\n      "url": "https://www.axios.com/2026/09/14/ai-stocks-apocalypse-wall-street"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "On Monday, September 14, 2026, major U.S. stock indexes closed lower, primarily driven by a global decline in AI-related stocks following industry leaders'' warnings about the need to slow development for humanity''s safety. Additionally, rising oil prices pushed the 10-year Treasury yield to 5% for the first time since 2023. Despite these factors, gains in non-AI sectors helped contain the losses on Wall Street. The S&P 500 dropped 0.5% (37 points) to 7,619.98, the Dow Jones Industrial Average declined 0.3% (152.09 points) to 52,421.20, and the Nasdaq composite fell 0.6% (146.62 points) to 26,186.41. The Russell 2000 index, tracking smaller companies, slipped 0.4% (11.71 points) to 2,892.24. Brent crude oil settled at $105.68 per barrel after approaching $110 earlier in the day. Despite the day''s declines, all major indexes remain significantly up for the year: the S&P 500 by 11.3%, Dow by 9.1%, Nasdaq by 12.7%, and Russell 2000 by 16.5%.",\n      "url": "https://apnews.com/article/8f72a301be85728018018735163f4dad"\n    },\n    {\n      "text": "Several U.S. states are reevaluating generous tax exemptions given to AI and tech companies for data center investments due to unexpectedly massive revenue losses. Ohio, for example, projected $136 million in lost revenue for 2026 but actually incurred $1.6 billion in losses—over 5 ','\n',char(10)),'[]',1,'gpt-4o-mini',0.011959999999999999431,'');
