is no dated, firm-specific NVDA catalyst inside the next trading-day window that is both new and actionable. The only confirmed fresh linkage is Nscale’s Sep. 18 IPO filing; its prospectus discloses NVIDIA’s planned investment, but the agreement dates to Sep. 15 and the indicated closing is around Nov. 16, so it is context rather than a Monday opening catalyst. [nscale_nvidia_sale]\n\nThe setup is constructive over weeks but not a reason to force a four-hour trade: NVDA is near the middle of its 20-day range [position_in_20d_range], above its 50-day mean [distance_from_50d_mean_pct], and has already advanced over the past five days [move_5d_pct]. Estimate revisions are overwhelmingly positive [eps_revisions_up_30d; eps_revisions_down_30d; eps_revision_balance], while valuation is materially lower on forward than trailing earnings [trailing_pe; forward_pe]. Those facts support the longer-term demand thesis, but they do not identify a new buyer for this session.\n\nFor the specified geometry, the measured no-news tape is unfavorable: stops occur first more than twice as often as targets [MEASURED BASE RATE]. With no qualifying catalyst, retain that base-rate forecast rather than invent an edge. The desk should veto entry rather than short a company with strong revisions and fundamentals or buy it after a recent move without a fresh trigger.','\n',char(10)),'','',NULL,1,6,8,'none',0.28899999999999996802,0.63499999999999996447,0.075999999999999996447,0.3,NULL,NULL,0.28909465020576128324,0.25,'No qualifying catalyst. The measured no-news pattern for this exact NVDA setup has a materially higher stop-first rate than target-first rate, while positive revisions and strong fundamentals make a discretionary short equally unattractive.','',0,'gpt-5.6-terra','flex',1,17524,1739,607,0.037957999999999998408,19.208437919616699218,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL);
